    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a low-fat mayo prototype and the emulsion keeps breaking when we drop the pH below 3.8. can you explain what's happening at the molecular level with the lecithin specifically in high-acid environments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In acidic environments, particularly below a pH of 3.8, the emulsifying properties of lecithin—the molecule responsible for stabilizing emulsions—deteriorate. Lecithin contains both hydrophobic and hydrophilic parts; in higher acidity, the carboxyl groups in lecithin become protonated, which reduces its ability to form a stable emulsion by hindering the interaction between the fatty acids in the oil phase and water molecules. This drop in interaction efficiency causes the emulsion to break, leading to phase separation. You may need to adjust your formulation to include pH stabilizers or explore alternative emulsifiers that can withstand lower pH values.",
      "source": "target",
