    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so the protonation basically messes with the charge of the head groups, right? if i were to switch to a more robust emulsifier like polydextrose or maybe increase the concentration of the lecithin to compensate for the instability, would that actually fix the phase separation or is the acid just going to keep disrupting the interface regardless?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Increasing the concentration of lecithin could indeed help, as more lecithin molecules can help to bridge the gap between the oil and water phases, thus maintaining the emulsion despite a slightly acidic environment. However, for a substantial pH range, like below 3.8, relying solely on lecithin won't be sufficient since the acidity affects the lecithin head groups heavily. Polydextrose, being more about sweetening than emulsifying, might not be compatible with this objective unless it's combined with other emulsifiers that protect the oil-water interface across a broader pH range. Consider looking into experts and professional emulsifiers designed for low pH applications, which are tailored to maintain stability across acidic environments.",
      "source": "target",
