    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "fair point on the polydextrose. so if the lecithin is getting \"disrupted\" by the acid, is it more of a sudden failure or is it actually a gradual degradation of the interfacial film? i'm trying to figure out if i'll see visible separation early in the shelf life or if it's going to happen all at once during storage.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The interfacial film dilution in an emulsion is not typically instantaneous; it tends to be a gradual process. As the pH drops, the lecithin becomes more protonated, and this gradual accumulation of protonated lecithin molecules at the oil-water interface would increasingly weaken the emulsion over time, leading to gradual separation rather than a sudden failure. However, the rate of this process depends on multiple factors, including the exact pH, the concentration of lecithin, and environmental conditions (light, temperature, etc.). Shelf life testing will help determine the exact time frame for phase separation to become visually obvious. So, it might slowly deteriorate over storage or it could maintain stability for a longer period, subject to exact conditions.",
      "source": "target",
